INT. TARA'S BEDROOM - MORNING

Tara sits on the edge of her bed. She is eight years old. She has two short bunches in her hair and wears bright striped socks. She pulls on a lilac raincoat.

Tara has generalised anxiety disorder. Her mind and body are highly sensitive to changes in her environment.

On her wooden desk sits a weather-house toy. It is painted purple and slate grey. A tiny wooden woman in a lilac dress stands outside the house. This means rain is coming.

Tara looks at her weather log. It is a notebook filled with drawings of clouds, wind directions, and daily barometric pressure charts. 

Tara wants to win the Golden Barometer badge at school today. It is the prize for the most accurate daily weather log. She needs one more perfect entry.

TARA
Today is the final entry. I need it to be perfect.

Tara checks the straps of her small backpack. She pulls them tight. She slides her weather-house toy carefully into the side pocket.

EXT. STREET - MORNING

Tara walks down the concrete sidewalk. She wears her lilac raincoat zipped up to her chin.

The morning sky is bright blue. No clouds are visible.

Suddenly, a dog barks loudly behind a wooden fence. 

Tara jumps. Her heart rate increases. 

A garbage truck rumbles past. It releases a loud hiss of air brakes.

Tara stops. Her body reacts to the sudden noises. Her chest feels tight. 

She stands still on the sidewalk. She grips her backpack straps tightly. She breathes in slowly through her nose. She breathes out through her mouth.

Tara looks down at the grass beside the path. She notices a line of black ants. They are carrying food up to a high, thick dirt mound.

TARA
(to herself)
The ants are building their walls high today. They only do that when heavy rain is coming.

Tara opens her weather log. She writes: "Ants building high. Air feels heavy."

INT. CLASSROOM - MORNING

The classroom is bright and warm.

Mr. Miller stands near the whiteboard. He is a friendly teacher in a blue sweater.

On Mr. Miller's desk sits the Golden Barometer badge. It is shiny, gold, and shaped like a cloud. Tara stares at it from her desk. She wants to earn it.

Her classmate, Chloe, sits next to her. Chloe is drawing doodles in a messy notebook.

Mr. Miller taps the whiteboard. An image of a smiling cartoon sun is projected on the screen.

MR. MILLER
According to the National Weather Service, we have clear skies all day. No rain. No wind. Just perfect sunshine for our afternoon science walk.

The class cheers. 

Tara looks out the window. The leaves on the maple tree are curling upward. The silver undersides of the leaves are showing.

Tara feels a prickle on the back of her neck. Her ears feel tight, like they do when she drives up a steep mountain.

Tara raises her hand.

TARA
Mr. Miller, the air pressure is dropping fast. The maple leaves are turning upside down. A storm is coming.

Chloe giggles from the next desk.

CHLOE
Tara, the computer says it is sunny. You are always worrying about something.

MR. MILLER
(gently)
Chloe is right about the forecast, Tara. There is no storm on the radar. You do not need to worry so much.

Tara looks down at her weather log. She feels the alarm in her chest. She knows her body is reacting to real changes. It is not a fault. It is her early-warning system.

INT. CLASSROOM - AFTERNOON

The wall clock ticks. It is one o'clock.

Tara sits at her desk. She feels the air grow thick and heavy.

She reaches into her backpack. Inside, she has a large purple tarp, three emergency whistles, and a roll of strong tape. She prepared these because her anxiety told her to be ready for any change.

Mr. Miller stands up and claps his hands.

MR. MILLER
Time for our outdoor science walk. Grab your notebooks, everyone.

EXT. SCHOOL PLAYGROUND - AFTERNOON

The class walks onto the grassy field behind the school.

The sun is still shining, but the light has a strange, yellow tint.

Tara walks at the back of the group. She keeps her lilac raincoat zipped up. She looks at the horizon.

She feels the wind shift. It is blowing from the east now. It smells like wet dirt and ozone.

Tara looks at the western sky. A thin line of dark slate grey clouds is rising fast.

TARA
Mr. Miller, we need to go back inside. The wind has shifted.

MR. MILLER
We have ten more minutes, Tara. Look, the sun is still out.

Suddenly, a massive gust of wind whips across the field. It blows Chloe's loose papers out of her hand.

The papers swirl high into the air.

The temperature drops ten degrees in a single second.

A loud rumble of thunder echoes across the field.

The sky turns dark slate grey. The sun disappears.

EXT. SCHOOL PLAYGROUND - CONTINUOUS

The wind whips the grass into waves.

MR. MILLER
(shouting)
Okay, everyone! Back to the building! Move quickly!

The kids run toward the main double doors of the school.

A massive flash of lightning lights up the sky.

CRACK!

A large branch from the old oak tree snaps. It crashes down directly in front of the main double doors.

The path is blocked by thick branches and sharp, broken wood.

The kids scream. They stop in their tracks.

Mr. Miller tries to pull at the branches, but they are too heavy and tangled.

MR. MILLER
The door is blocked! We need to go to the side entrance!

The wind howls. Dust and dry leaves fly into the kids' faces.

Chloe is crying. Leo, a quiet boy, freezes in the middle of the grass. He covers his ears with both hands.

LEO
I cannot move! I am scared!

EXT. SCHOOL PLAYGROUND - CONTINUOUS

Tara's chest is tight, but her mind is active. Her hypervigilance has prepared her for this exact moment.

She knows what to do. She takes charge.

Tara runs to Leo. She pulls her emergency whistle from her backpack.

She blows the whistle. It makes a sharp, loud sound that cuts through the wind.

The kids stop screaming and look at her.

TARA
Follow me! The side gym door is clear! I checked the latch this morning!

Tara grabs Leo's hand. He looks at her, trembling.

TARA
(to Leo)
Hold onto my hand. We are going to walk together. I have you.

Tara pulls the large purple tarp from her backpack. She hands one end to Chloe.

TARA
Chloe, help me hold this over our heads! It will block the wind and the branches!

Chloe grabs the tarp. Together, they hold it up. It forms a sturdy shield against the flying leaves and the first heavy drops of rain.

Tara leads the line of classmates across the windy field.

She watches the trees carefully. Her eyes scan the ground for deep puddles and loose wires. Her early-warning system keeps them moving safely.

INT. SCHOOL GYMNASIUM - CONTINUOUS

Tara pushes the heavy metal gym door open. The kids file inside quickly.

The door slams shut behind them, blocking out the wind.

They are safe inside. The gym is warm.

Outside, heavy rain beats against the high glass windows. Thunder shakes the walls.

The kids sit on the wooden bleachers, catching their breath.

Mr. Miller wraps a dry towel around Leo's shoulders.

Chloe walks over to Tara. She is still holding the corner of the purple tarp.

CHLOE
You knew exactly what to do. How did you know to bring a tarp and whistles?

TARA
My anxiety makes me think of what could happen. So I prepare.

Mr. Miller walks over to Tara. He holds the Golden Barometer badge in his hand.

MR. MILLER
The official forecast missed this storm completely, Tara. But your observations were correct. Your weather log was perfect, and your preparation kept everyone safe.

Mr. Miller pins the Golden Barometer badge to Tara's lilac raincoat.

The classmates clap and cheer. Tara smiles. She feels proud of her early-warning system.

INT. TARA'S BEDROOM - EVENING

The storm has passed. A gentle rain taps softly against the glass.

Tara sits on her bed. She still wears her lilac raincoat with the golden badge pinned to the front.

She holds her weather-house toy in her lap.

The tiny wooden woman is still standing outside the house, enjoying the damp air.

Suddenly, a loud emergency siren wails in the distance.

Tara's body reacts. Her heart beats faster. Her chest feels tight.

She does not try to stop the feeling. She accepts it as her natural early-warning system.

Tara reaches down and checks the straps of her backpack. She pulls them tight.

She is ready for whatever comes next.